1.4.m:
And on the subject of capacity, there are these verses:
What remains beyond the food and clothing of one's family is to be given;
otherwise, a giver's righteousness becomes sweet at first like honey, but poison in the end.

Nārada said:
Then I, having said to Dharmavarman, “Let the wealth stay with you;
I will take it at the time of need,” went to Mount Raivata.

Viśvakarman, a temple liṅga, and the name “Southern”;
Vibhīṣaṇa, one made of dust, and the name “Best Friend”;
Sagara, one made of bamboo shoots, and the name “United”;

The daitya said:
I shall perform dreadful austerity for the sake of my wife, O Grandfather;
and I am resolved for the sake of a son who will destroy the pride of the gods.

The woman said:
“Know me to be the Fortune of the three worlds, O best of Daityas;
I have been won by your austerity or by your prowess, O lord.

Meṣa became the ruler of wind, and Kujambha became the giver of wealth;
and Tāraka assigned the offices of the other [gods] to Daityas.
Thus ends the twenty-first chapter, entitled 'Description of the Victory of Tāraka' in the War between Gods and Asuras, in the Kaumārikākhaṇḍa of the first section, the Māheśvarakhaṇḍa, in the Mahāpurāṇa of eighty-one thousand verses, the glorious Skandapurāṇa.

Your dwelling is in the cremation ground; you have no shame because of your nakedness;
and pitilessness from holding a skull—who could bear you like this?"
Thus ends the twenty-seventh chapter, entitled "The Description of Pārvatī's Great Anger", in the Kumāreśvaramāhātmya of the Kaumārikākhaṇḍa, in the first Māheśvarakhaṇḍa, in the glorious Skanda Mahāpurāṇa consisting of eighty-one thousand verses.

And spoke thus: "O fool, I shall kill you! Behold my power!
You are the root of the gods, of dharmas, and of the wise;
having slain you today, I shall cut down all of them! Behold my power today!"

Then the gods led by Hari and Indra sang praises, and the foremost apsarases led by Rambhā danced;
playing all kinds of musical instruments, the immortals chanted repeatedly, "Well done! Well done!"
Thus ends the thirty-second chapter, titled "Description of the Slaying of Tāraka by Kumāra" in the Kumāreśvaramāhātmya within the Kaumārikākhaṇḍa of the first section, the Māheśvarakhaṇḍa, in the glorious Skanda Purāṇa, the great Purāṇa in eighty-one thousand verses.

Mahākāla said:
These three gods are of boundless majesty, O bull among men;
even the minds of the lords among yogins are bewildered here, so what of mine?

Wealth should be acquired righteously, and one should also sacrifice with effort.
A wise man should never address even an inferior with 'thou';
addressing with 'thou' or slaying—both are equal in the case of gurus.
